## 1. Projections – five ways to read a state

The math says from each `State` we can extract five different views.  
Here we define them:

```rust
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Projections – five different "views" of a state
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

impl State {
    /// Projection 1: list of point coordinates (raw layer)
    pub fn projection_coords(&self) -> Vec<Point> {
        self.layer.clone()
    }

    /// Projection 2: adjacency matrix (who connects to whom) – here we have only
    /// connections from previous layer to this one, but we can also compute
    /// connections from this layer to the next if we have the next state.
    /// For this projection we treat the prev_connections as a matrix.
    pub fn projection_adjacency(&self, total_prev: usize) -> Vec<Vec<bool>> {
        let n = total_prev;
        let m = self.layer.len();
        let mut mat = vec![vec![false; m]; n];
        for (from, to) in &self.prev_connections {
            if *from < n && *to < m {
                mat[*from][*to] = true;
            }
        }
        mat
    }

    /// Projection 3: distance matrix (distances from each point to every other)
    pub fn projection_distance_matrix(&self) -> Vec<Vec<f64>> {
        let n = self.layer.len();
        let mut mat = vec![vec![0.0; n]; n];
        for i in 0..n {
            for j in 0..n {
                mat[i][j] = self.layer[i].distance(&self.layer[j]);
            }
        }
        mat
    }

    /// Projection 4: angular distribution (angles of points relative to origin)
    pub fn projection_angles(&self) -> Vec<f64> {
        self.layer.iter()
            .map(|p| p.y.atan2(p.x))
            .collect()
    }

    /// Projection 5: radial distribution (radii from origin)
    pub fn projection_radii(&self) -> Vec<f64> {
        self.layer.iter()
            .map(|p| (p.x.powi(2) + p.y.powi(2)).sqrt())
            .collect()
    }
}
